course: euphemisms
lesson: 38
title: Soft words for hard things: rudeness and swearing
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: When people stub a toe or drop a plate, a swear word wants to jump out. Polite people catch it halfway and turn it into a harmless word that starts with the same sound.
explain: That is why a mum who drops the shopping may cry "Sugar!" or "Shoot!": she started the rude s-word and swerved into something sweet or something about a gun.
explain: Words like "freaking", "flipping" and "effing" work the same way for the strongest swear word, the f-word, so the speaker sounds cross but not filthy.
explain: Some soft words just name the bad word without saying it: "the s-word", "the f-word". Others, like "blimey" and "strewth", were once oaths about God that have worn soft with time.
explain: The softer word keeps the feeling (anger, surprise, pain) but drops the shock.
fact: "Drat!" is an old-fashioned mild word used instead of "damn" when annoyed.
fact: "Dash it all!" is an old-fashioned British way of saying "damn it all".
fact: "Blast!" is a British mild word used instead of "damn" when something goes wrong.
fact: "Heck" is a mild word used instead of "hell", as in "What the heck?"
fact: "Blimey!" is a British cry of surprise that comes from the old oath "God blind me".
fact: "Strewth!" is an Australian cry of surprise that comes from "God's truth".
fact: "Holy cow!" is an American cry of surprise used instead of a ruder oath.
fact: "Holy moly!" is a jokey cry of surprise used instead of a ruder oath.
fact: "Son of a gun" is an American soft form of a much ruder insult; it can show annoyance or even friendly surprise.
fact: "Shoot!" is an American mild word used instead of the s-word, a rude word for poo.
fact: "Sugar!" is a British mild word used instead of the s-word.
fact: "The s-word" is a polite way to name a rude swear word for poo without saying it.
fact: "Fudge!" is a mild word used instead of the f-word.
fact: "Frick" is American slang used instead of the f-word.
fact: "Freaking" is a slangy stand-in for the f-word used as "very" or to show anger, as in "freaking cold".
fact: "Frigging" is a slangy stand-in for the f-word, used to show anger.
fact: "Flipping" is a British mild stand-in for "bloody" or the f-word, as in "flipping freezing".
fact: "Effing" is British slang that says only the first sound of the f-word swear.
fact: "Eff off" is a rude British way to tell someone to go away, softened from the f-word.
fact: "The f-word" is a polite way to name the strongest common English swear word without saying it.
exercise: What does "Drat!" mean when Grandma loses her glasses? | A mild "damn", showing she is annoyed | She has seen a big rat in the kitchen cupboard | She is calling the cat to come | She has found her glasses again
exercise: An old English gentleman says "Dash it all!" What is he saying? | A polite "damn it all" | Let us run quickly | Draw a line under it | Pour the drink out
exercise: What is "Blast!" usually when a British person drops something? | A mild word instead of "damn" | A warning that a bomb is near | The sound of a loud radio | A shout to start a rocket
exercise: What does "What the heck?" stand in for? | What the hell? | What the hat? | What the deck? | What the neck?
exercise: Where does the British cry "Blimey!" come from? | An old oath "God blind me" | The name of a fruit, lime | A sailor's word for a ship | A Scottish word for rain
exercise: An Australian says "Strewth!" What does it come from? | "God's truth" | "Straw" | "Stretch" | "Strong youth"
exercise: What does "Holy cow!" show? | Surprise | A love of cows | Prayer in a church | Hunger for beef
exercise: What does "Holy moly!" show? | Surprise, said jokingly | A holy person | A small animal | A kind of sauce
exercise: An American dad says "Well, son of a gun!" when his team scores. What is it? | A soft form of a rude phrase, showing surprise | He is calling his son a soldier in the army | He is talking about his gun | He is angry at his son
exercise: An American kid drops ice cream and says "Shoot!" What is "shoot" standing in for? | The rude s-word | An order to fire a gun | A shot at goal | A new plant growing
exercise: A British mum says "Oh, sugar!" when she burns the toast. What does she mean? | A soft form of the rude s-word | She wants sugar on the toast | She calls her child sweetheart | She has run out of sugar
exercise: What is "the s-word"? | A polite way to name a rude swear word | The word "sorry", said after a mistake | A secret password | The word "stupid"
exercise: Dad hits his thumb and says "Fudge!" What is "fudge" standing in for? | The f-word | A sweet he wants | A burnt cake | The word "fool"
exercise: What is "frick" a stand-in for? | The f-word | A fridge | A trick | A brick
exercise: "It's freaking cold!" What is "freaking" doing here? | Standing in for the f-word to mean "very" | Saying the cold is a strange freak of nature | Saying that the cold scares people | Saying someone is acting strangely
exercise: What is "frigging" a stand-in for? | The f-word | The fridge | A frog | Friday
exercise: In "It's flipping freezing", what is "flipping"? | A mild stand-in for "bloody" or the f-word | Turning a pancake over in the pan for breakfast | Doing a somersault | Throwing a coin
exercise: What is "effing" in "this effing printer"? | A stand-in that says only the first sound of the f-word | A brand name for printers made in the United States | The name of the letter F | A word for a broken machine
exercise: What does "Eff off!" mean? | Go away, said rudely | Turn the light off | Take your shoes off | Go to sleep now
exercise: What is "the f-word"? | A polite way to name the strongest common swear word | The word "friend", used between close mates at school | The word "fail" | The word "fine"
test: TV news: "The minister was heard muttering the f-word into a live microphone." What happened? | He said a strong swear word | He said the word "friend" | He forgot a word | He spoke French
test: "omg sugar sugar SUGAR i've locked the keys in the car" Is she talking about food? | No, it is a soft swear because she is upset | Yes, she wants some sugar | Yes, the keys are hidden in the sugar bowl | No, she means she is very happy
test: Little brother says "shoot" is a swear and he's telling Mum. right? | No, it is a mild stand-in, not a real swear | Yes, it is the worst word in English | Yes, it is about guns so it is banned at school | No, it means "well done"
test: "What the heck is that smell?" How strong is "heck"? | Mild: it stands in for "hell" | Very strong: worse than any word | Not a swear at all: it means "neck" | Medium: it is a word for a farm
test: "eff off and leave my bike alone." What does the speaker want? | The other person to go away now | The bike to be switched off | The other person to ride the bike | The bike to be fixed
test: An Aussie farmer sees a huge spider and says "Strewth!" What does he feel? | Surprise | Joy | Boredom | Hunger
test: "Holy moly, you ate the whole cake??" What is the speaker feeling? | Surprise | Holiness | Hunger | Sleepiness
test: A teacher writes: "Sam told a classmate to 'eff off' at lunch." What did Sam do wrong? | He rudely told a classmate to go away | He switched off the lights in the dining hall | He took off his shoes at lunch | He went home early
test: Grandpa, reading old letters: "Dash it all, I've lost my place!" Which modern word means the same? | Damn | Hurry | Draw | Run
test: A boss texts: "the frigging server is down AGAIN". How does the boss feel? | Very annoyed | Cold | Calm | Glad
test: "this freaking homework, no wait, it's actually fine, i'm just tired." What is the speaker's mood at the end? | Calmer, just tired | Still very angry | Scared of homework | Excited and happy
test: Why does someone say "Fudge!" instead of the word it stands for? | To show anger without using a strong swear word | Because they are thinking about sweets | Because fudge is a stronger swear | Because it is the f-word in French
exam: Identify the oath from which the exclamation "blimey" is held to derive. | God blind me | Blame me | Blind man's buff | Lime and lemon
exam: Ascertain the expression for which the interjection "heck" constitutes a softened substitute. | Hell | Help | Neck | Hack
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established meaning of the phrase "the s-word". | A circumlocution naming a vulgar swear word without uttering it | An abbreviation of the word "sorry" | A password employed by secret societies of the nineteenth century | A formal synonym for the word "stupid"
exam: Where an individual exclaims "Sugar!" upon suffering a misfortune, determine the principal function of the utterance. | To express vexation whilst avoiding a vulgar oath | To request a sweetener for a beverage | To address a loved one affectionately | To report the depletion of household provisions
